8. DYNAMIC DOM DETECTION

Modern websites frequently create, replace, hide, or modify elements after initial page loading.

Implement dynamic discovery.

Use MutationObserver where appropriate.

Observe relevant changes, including:

- Child element insertion.
- Child element removal.
- Attribute changes.
- Role changes.
- Visibility-related changes.
- Disabled state changes.
- Newly inserted forms.
- Newly inserted controls.
- Shadow host insertion.
- Relevant iframe insertion.

Avoid rescanning the entire page for every small DOM mutation.

Implement:

- Debouncing.
- Batched scanning.
- Mutation filtering.
- Deduplication.
- Incremental updates.
- Bounded queues.
- Cancellation.
- Observer cleanup.

Avoid observing irrelevant attributes or generating an infinite mutation loop.

The scanner must not modify the page merely to observe it.

Dynamic element lifecycle

Elements may be:

- Added.
- Removed.
- Replaced.
- Hidden.
- Shown.
- Disabled.
- Re-enabled.
- Moved to another container.

Track element identity carefully.

A removed element must not remain incorrectly marked as a valid current target.

If a locator becomes stale, mark it stale and require rediscovery.

Do not silently reuse a detached element reference.

