9. LONG PAGES AND OFF-SCREEN ELEMENTS

The scanner must discover elements beyond the currently visible viewport.

It must not restrict discovery to elements visible on the screen.

Support:

- Long forms.
- Long pages.
- Lazy-loaded content.
- Elements below the fold.
- Nested scroll containers.
- Dynamically expanded sections.
- Elements revealed after scrolling.

Passive scanning

First, perform a non-destructive DOM inspection of the currently loaded document.

Do not automatically scroll the page during passive scanning.

Do not change the user's scroll position.

Do not click controls simply to reveal hidden content.

Explicit extended scanning

Where additional content is loaded only after scrolling, support an optional extended scan.

The extended scan must:

1. Save the current scroll position.
2. Identify relevant scroll containers.
3. Move through the page in bounded increments only when authorized.
4. Allow lazy-loaded content to appear.
5. Discover newly inserted elements.
6. Avoid clicking or submitting controls.
7. Restore the user's original scroll position.
8. Stop when the scan reaches its configured limit.
9. Support cancellation.
10. Clean up temporary observers and listeners.

Never run an unbounded scroll loop.

Do not automatically expand every accordion, menu, or dialog.

