15. SCANNER EXECUTION ARCHITECTURE

Keep the scanner independent of the UI layer.

The UI must not directly implement DOM traversal.

Use a clear separation between:

- JavaScript DOM discovery.
- Native bridge communication.
- Kotlin scanner coordinator.
- Element normalization.
- Locator generation.
- Scan result storage or delivery.
- HUD presentation.

Reuse the existing JavaScript bridge architecture where possible.

Session isolation

Every scan must have an identifiable scan session.

Bind scanning to:

- Scan session ID.
- Initiating tab ID.
- Expected WebView instance.
- Current document/navigation context.
- Canonical origin where applicable.

Validate incoming scanner callbacks.

Ignore stale callbacks.

Prevent events from one tab from entering another tab's scan results.

Handle navigation and WebView replacement safely.

Reuse the existing Phase 4A isolation principles.

JavaScript bridge

The bridge must:

- Accept structured scan results.
- Validate session ownership.
- Validate tab ownership.
- Reject stale callbacks.
- Handle malformed JSON safely.
- Enforce payload size limits.
- Avoid logging sensitive data.
- Support scan cancellation.
- Support observer cleanup.

Do not expose unrestricted native functionality to page JavaScript.

